== Cadmium

The element cadmium is called after the Greek word 'kadmeia'. This is the ancient name for calamine, zinc carbonate.

Cadmium, with atomic number 48, has an atomic weight of 112.41. Its melting point is 320.9 °C and the boiling point is 765 °C. The specific gravity of the element is 8.65.

The element was discovered in 1817. Stromeyer found it as an impurity in zinc carbonate. Cadmium most often occurs in small quantities associated with zinc ores such as sphalerite (#ch("ZnS")). Greenockite (#ch("CdS")) is the only mineral of any consequence bearing cadmium.

Almost all cadmium is obtained as a by-product in the treatment of zinc, copper and lead ores. This fact that cadmium is a component in zinc ores hasn't always been distinguished in the past. The way how zinc metal was produced in an industrial way was started in the beginning of the 19th century. Knowing now that the cadmium, present in the zinc ores, was also reduced to the cadmium metal, it escaped during the process. Now, more than a hundred years later, this uncontrolled part of the process is a serious source of environmental contamination.

Calamine mines were already explored for centuries in some European countries for the production of brass. So also those mines near Moresnet in the Altenberg (la Vieille Montagne) in Belgium. On March 24, 1806 the chemist Abbé J. J. D. Dony from Liège was the new owner. In 1808 he started with the production of metallic zinc using horizontal retorts in St. Leonard, a suburb of Liège. Due to a lack of capital and business view, the main part of this company was sold to Dominique Mosselman, a businessman from Brussels. This new alliance was the base for the founding of the 'Société des Mines et fonderies de La Vieille Montagne' in 1837.#super[@lit:meindert-stokroos-zink]

The three developed processes for the manufacturing of metallic zinc are all based on the very same principle, viz. the heating of calamine with cokes; a distillation of zinc.

#narrow[
The 'English method' is called after the method developed by William Champion. A patent was granted to him in 1739. According to this method, the first zinc kilns were built in Warmley near Bristol in 1734.

The 'Liège method' with horizontal retorts started in 1809 but became of any importance only after $plus.minus$ 1840.

The 'Silesian method', using half cylindrical shaped retorts was developed by Johann Christian Ruhberg in 1798.
]

A fundamental change in processing was the development of the electrolytic manufacturing of zinc. The first patents were granted already in 1880 but it became important for the industry after $plus.minus$ 1920. If one considers that e.g. the zinc factory as the one in Budel (North Brabant - The Netherlands), which was founded in 1892 by Lucien and Emil Dor and started production with only 8 small kilns according to the 'Liège method', switched over to the electrolytic process in 1973, one can imagine how much cadmium is also 'produced' and released through the chimney and the slags. These slags were given to the local farmers and used for elevation of local roads etc. The community is now confronted with the fact that a great number of hectares are contaminated with the toxic dust from this 170,000 tons zinc p/a producing factory.#super[@lit:de-volkskrant-23111984]

The world production of cadmium amounts 15,000 to 20,000 tons p/a.#super[@lit:cadmium-world-mineral] More than half of this amount is delivered by the USA, Mexico and Canada. Also Russia is an important producer. A minor amount is delivered now by Belgium, France and Germany.

Cadmium is used in thin metal coatings to prevent it from atmospherical influences. As metallic material it forms the negative pole in nickel-cadmium batteries. Cadmium is also a component in some low-melting eutectic alloys as solders for lead.

The brown coloured cadmium oxide #ch("CdO") can be obtained by the combustion and oxidizing of the metal. Together with cadmium carbonate (#ch("CdCO3"), white powder) it is the most important raw material source for the manufacturing of #ch("CdSe") glazes. The yellow cadmium sulfide (#ch("CdS")) occurs in nature as greenockite but it is chemically produced by precipitation from solutions of cadmium salts. After precipitation it has a cubic structure but after calcining it has a hexagonal crystal structure which makes it a stable pigment. The cadmium pigments do have very good properties for application in polymers. This has always been by far the most important application of cadmium pigments what is also expressed in @tab:application-cadmium-pigments showing the use of cadmium pigments in Germany in 1981.#super[@lit:k-heinle-g]

