The toxicity of cadmium is already subject of all kind of investigations, regulations, discussions and restrictions for use after the Itai-itai disease in Japan became a world-issue. Mining in the Toyama Prefecture by the Mitsui Mining and Smelting Co. was already done since long times. The Kamioka Mines in Toyama increased the output of the mines due to the wartime demands for zinc, lead and other metals caused by the Russian Japanese war and World War I. The output increased significantly before World War II and so did the cadmium contamination. The waste water contaminated the Jinzu river and all water what was used for preparing food, washing, drinking, fishing and irrigation of the rice fields.

The main cause of the disease was determined in 1955 by Dr. Hagino followed by investigations of the Toyama prefecture in 1961. In 1968 a statement was issued by the Ministry of Health and Welfare mentioning the symptoms of Itai-itai disease caused by cadmium poisoning. The cadmium poisoning is mainly causing weakening of the bones and kidney failure.

Various conferences were held on national, European and a world-wide scale focusing on cadmium with the unavoidable outcome to come to minimizing the application of cadmium with only some minor exceptions. During the 80ties and 90ties of last century the application of cadmium for most applications is forbidden and laid down in laws. Only some exceptions are made were it is technically not possible to substitute the cadmium by other alternatives. Ceramic colour application is e.g. still an exception.
